**b)** 
Assume now that $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ is unknown. We use the conjugate prior presented in Chapter 3 of the book
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{\theta} \vert \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \sim \mathrm{N}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_0,\boldsymbol{\Sigma}/\kappa_0) \\
    \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \sim \mathrm{IW}(\nu_0, \nu_0 \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_0),
\end{align}
where $\nu_0$ is the degrees of freedom and $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_0$ is our best guess of $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ a priori. Use the non-informative choice $\nu_0 = 2$ and set
$$
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_0 = 
\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1.5\\
1.5 & 4
\end{array}\right).
$$



